films and photographs are what  introduced us to the famous earthworks  of the American Southwest having  produced the sights auras their  documentation inspired us to make the  pilgrimage but they also produced  expectations that were confounded by  experience often on our journey we found  photographs to be forbidden or futile  what do film and photography leave out  smell scale our own feelings at the site  the histories of expropriation buried  under the surface these things seem to  be beyond our documentary grasp what we  offer then is a travel log of the  undocumented  the sheer flatness of the Great Salt  Lake that greeted us from the plane  utterly flat deadly flat was sensory  deprivation pure flat then white then  pink at the edge and Bryan almost  imagined  the receding pink water of the Great  Salt Lake made the jetty look a little  forgotten long cracks in the soil made  this forgotten feeling even stronger  some three or four dead pelicans around  the site appeared crystallized in time  the scorching dry air kept things from  rotting I could see some salt crystals  underneath one of the dead Birds the  death looks so undisturbed here like the  site itself  from the Spiral Jetty to the sun tunnels  it was 116 miles of nothing  repetitive stretches of brown and green  my mother would have called its credit  dear Albanian for this great expanse  wasteland a big nothing  we traveled so many miles while seeing  so little we spent more time in the  vastness between sites than in the sites  themselves as we arrived to the Sun  tunnels the bus LED and air in sand we  wanted to see the work before the sky  turned dark and we arrived just before  the sunset there Peter taught us that if  you just hold your fist out toward the  Sun you can measure how much time you  have before it sets one fist from the  horizon to the Sun gives you about one  hour  driving to double negative  I kept envisioning a particular image a  photograph of an artist I worked for  taken two decades earlier  moments after he ignited a handheld  explosive forming a small mushroom cloud  in the air he used the iconic view of  double negative the two subtractions  aligned with the curving Mesa and with  his cloud he made the work his own I  decided to do the same Ryanair agreed to  take the photograph though this was the  only thing that went my way I was  without a stockpile of firecrackers and  gunpowder forced to improvise I decided  to set a small fire though I had flipped  through pages and pages of archival  photographs documenting the mini trials  of this project nothing prepared me for  the wind I had never thought of double-  as a place but rather always as an image  the wind put out the fires I tried to  light indifferent to my ambition and  will by the time I stood at the exact  spot where my boss had created the image  the sky had turned cloudy and purple the  wind was so strong I was afraid that I  would blow into the void  to document the universe one must invent  a representation of infinity and in turn  failed to represent its true enormity it  is impossible to directly capture the  Very Large Array much as VLA zone  attempt to visualize the universe is  merely a highly mediated translation of  radio signals into images throughout the  trip we often felt that the vastness of  the landscape swallowed the projects of  man but the array was an exception it  dominated the landscape  it's not as if we could have captured  the missile-launch we accidentally  witnessed when we visited White Sands  Missile Range it happened too quickly it  was too awesome nor the somber weight of  trendy side or the first nuclear weapon  was detonated anyway no photographs were  allowed the activity there was  confidential and governmental there was  one exception though the Oryx an  antelope species native to Africa but  imported to New Mexico to increase game  tourism they now thrive in white sands a  landscape characterized by desolation  and destruction the Oryx is punctuated  moments of mourning moments of the  technological sublime in moments of  trepidation as our bodies encountered  remnants of a nuclear explosion  upon arrival of Acoma Pueblo we were  told by our guide that videos were  prohibited and photos were to be taken  only unauthorized devices an  understandable practice given the  community's historical tendency towards  secrecy in the service of  self-preservation we were surprised  though when Reiner having just pulled  out a pen and notebook was informed that  writing too was forbidden sitting with  this rule I felt a strange splitting of  the senses my attention drawn to but the  weight of language and knowing and the  frailty of the image I hadn't realized  that Lightning field encompassed such a  vast area four hundred stainless steel  poles measuring one mile by one  kilometer the poles could only be seen  frontally from the horizontal but what  did lightning field look like from above  did it's razor sharp points recede into  the air at a certain height render the  silvery grid invisible imagine a  nocturnal flash and then for an instant  all of it illuminated in plain sight the  occasional Blackfoot Daisy bloomed in  front of de Maria's techno sculptures  hairs and Jackrabbits nibbled from low  shrubs leaping with sprigs in their  mouths photo energy the catalyst which  transforms sunlight into sugar is the  same element which allows lightning  field to be captured on film a treat  we crossed state after state time zone  after time zone and nudged right up into  the us-mexico border before we finally  made it to Marfa we befriended strangers  at Prada Marfa as we took turns  photographing one another it's only so  isolated  apparently we waited ten minutes in the  dark before the Flavin piece was turned  on then suddenly we were transported to  a thicken world breathing peer magenta  and forest green we were asked not to  take photos so as to encourage us to be  present to keep the tour moving and so  we fought the magnetic attraction of our  hands to the camera phones in our  pockets but the impulse proved too  strong we couldn't resist making a few  the block a portion of Donald Judd's  sixty thousand three hundred forty acres  of Texan land full of countless  meticulously arranged objects we were  constantly reminded of this as we  strolled through beautifully staged  rooms that served as the backdrop to  Donald Judd's life though its scale and  obsessive noeleen may suggest otherwise  the space is intimate subtle  misalignments of the objects are  evocative of this potential for life for  entropy the tools helped us imagine what  dad used to do here how he used to live  here  no photographs allowed such declaratives  and demands reminded us that this place  was once meant for life in a sense the  undocumented all converge with the over  documented we could take the memory of  these objects and sites home but nothing  else  medium dimension author these specific  details would be published in the  catalog resume responsible for telling  the definitive narrative of jud efforts  to create definitive narratives through  controlled representation and judgy and  meticulousness reparent and several of  the sites we visited well the government  authored a particular narrative of white  sands de maria so rigidly controlled the  representation of his lightning field  but these falsely singular eyes  narratives became confounded in the  spaces between the official  representations and dark experience  prior to our trip images defined our  knowledge of these sites selected images  controlled the reception meaning and  representation of these works but on  site we were unable to deny their  material spatial significance the  uncontestable eyes Dobb jecht swallowed  by the messy and complicated histories  and landscapes which were so easily  pushed to the background in editorials  and artist statements here it was plain  to see that the efforts control  reception meaning and representation  were futile  you
 

